  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings and prevention strategies are based on driving simulator experiments rather than real-world highway driving data.",
      "what": "The study developed a risk-field-guided quantitative framework to assess and mitigate car-following risks on foggy bridges using driving simulation experiments across three scenarios: ordinary roads, ordinary bridges, and foggy bridges. Risk field force models were constructed to quantify driver behavior, revealing that foggy bridges induce excessive following tendencies with insufficient behavioral compensation, leading to the highest risk levels among the tested environments. A targeted prevention strategy was also designed and validated to reduce these risks.",
      "why": "It quantifies how the combined environmental stressors of bridge infrastructure and fog impair driver cognition and destabilize car-following behavior, providing a validated intervention strategy for active bridge safety management."
    }
  },
